line.md\n@@ -1,6 +1,6 @@\n-# Outline (rev 3) — one SHAP run per instance: when is it a reliable basis for the conclusion?\n+# Outline (rev 4) — one SHAP run per instance: when is it a reliable basis for the conclusion?\n \n-Source of truth for the claims below: the `Brief` (idea-repo wiki, rev 3). Fixed definitions (players, value function, output space, conclusions C1–C3, P_c / P_c^ε / A, noise floors a–c, reference classes E1–E3) are **taken from the Brief unchanged**; this page says what the paper argues, which experiment carries each claim, and which figure shows it. It says what to test, not what the result must be: every claim is written so that its negation is an admissible, reportable outcome.\n+Source of truth for the claims below: the `Brief` (idea-repo wiki, rev 4). Fixed definitions (players, value function, output space, conclusions C1–C3, P_c / P_c^ε / A, noise floors a–c, reference classes E1–E3) are **taken from the Brief unchanged**; this page says what the paper argues, which experiment carries each claim, and which figure shows it. It says what to test, not what the result must be: every claim is written so that its negation is an admissible, reportable outcome.\n \n ## 1. Working title and one-sentence thesis\n *Is one run enough? Decision-level reliability of single-run SHAP attributions on tabular models.*\n@@ -21,7 +21,7 @@ Each claim has a *carrier experiment*, a *what would refute it* and a *status la\n |---|---|---|---|---|\n | **M1** | Randomness of the pipeline can be separated from the definition of the attribution; the exact reference of the same value function is available for every main cell, and the pipeline's random part is identified (extreme-size enumeration, d ≤ 10 exact default, LR zero variance). | Protocol + sanity checks: LR zero-variance control (E2); shap default exact at d ≤ 10; unbiasedness check vs E1/E2 for the paired/unbiased and permutation streams and for Kernel SHAP with `l1_reg=False` (with `l1_reg=\"auto\"` the default is not unbiased when m < 0.2·2^d, so it is checked and reported separately); reference-class table with model accuracy (AUC). | LR control shows variance; estimator biased vs reference at high budget (then report error to reference as bias + noise). | Contribution 1 |\n | **M2 (headline)** | At the default / deployment budget (B_def=50 random training rows), the share of instances whose single-run top-k (k=3) is correct (P_c^ε ≥ 0.9) is X% per dataset×model, with CI; the distribution of P_c across instances is wide (or narrow). | E-main: grid over budget m, n≈60–100 instances, R=100/60 repeats, E1/E2 references. | Not predicted; every number is a finding. Reported also at ε=0, k∈{1,3,5}, thresholds 0.8/0.95 (sensitivity grid). | C2, headline |\n-| **M3** | Aggregate agreement (mean correlation / relative L2) is high while decision-level reliability is lower (H1). | Same runs; both metrics per cell. Aggregate metrics and the reading of \"high\" fixed now: mean per-instance Pearson correlation and relative L2 between a run and the exact φ; \"aggregate high\" = mean Pearson ≥ 0.9 (a guess, replaced by the distribution of results, with sensitivity at 0.8/0.95). H1 is decided by the paired difference between the aggregate-high cells' decision-level share and 1, with CI. | Aggregate and decision metrics agree (then H1 is refuted and stated). | H1 |\n+| **M3** | Aggregate agreement (mean correlation / relative L2) is high while decision-level reliability is lower (H1). | Same runs; both metrics per cell. Aggregate metrics and the reading of \"high\" fixed now: mean per-instance Pearson correlation and relative L2 between a run and the exact φ; \"aggregate high\" = mean Pearso\n[truncated for publication]", "exit": null}}}, {"id": "7bfc5c61-0358-5dbe-bdc6-f856549948b4", "at": 1795638883.3848734, "clock": "host", "stream": "runner", "sequence": 31346, "layer": "worker", "category": "usage", "kind": "session.usage", "role": "paper-repo", "actor": "paper-repo", "executio